          "bbox": [
            0.635,
            0.042,
            0.848,
            0.058
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "How Forces Affect Motion"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "header",
          "index": 1,
          "bbox": [
            0.857,
            0.018,
            0.952,
            0.088
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "111 N"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 2,
          "bbox": [
            0.109,
            0.082,
            0.624,
            0.104
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "6.7 Forces Acting on a System of Objects"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 3,
          "bbox": [
            0.108,
            0.106,
            0.775,
            0.175
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Until now, you studied the three laws of Newton as applied to a single object. These laws allowed you to predict the position and velocities of the object as forces act on it. But can we apply these laws to two or more objects connected together?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 4,
          "bbox": [
            0.108,
            0.175,
            0.776,
            0.294
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Consider two boxes of masses "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "m _ { \\mathrm { 1 } }"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " and "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "m _ { 2 }"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " placed on a frictionless horizontal surface and connected by a string (Fig. 6.34). A force F pulls Box 1 to the right. Box 1 applies a force on Box 2 via the string. By Newton’s third law, Box 2 applies an equal and opposite force via the string on Box 1. We call this force tension T. On Box 1, the force F acts to the right, while the tension force "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "T"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " acts to the left. On Box 2, the tension force T acts on the right. How can we find the acceleration of each box?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "index": 5,
          "bbox": [
            0.271,
            0.299,
            0.612,
            0.362
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "image_body",
              "index": 5,
              "bbox": [
                0.271,
                0.299,
                0.612,
                0.362
              ],
              "image_path": "images/page_17_image_5.jpg",
              "content": ""
            },
            {
              "type": "image_caption",
              "index": 6,
              "bbox": [
                0.222,
                0.367,
                0.662,
                0.384
              ],
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Fig. 6.34: A system of two boxes attached together by a string"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 7,
          "bbox": [
            0.108,
            0.39,
            0.776,
            0.526
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "One approach is to calculate the net force on each box separately and then use Newton’s second law to find acceleration. A simpler way is to consider the two boxes and the string as a single "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "system",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": ". In this approach, the forces within the system "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "(internal forces)",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " need not be considered and only forces that act from the outside "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "(external forces)",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " matter. In our case, the tension "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "T"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " acting on both the boxes is the internal force, while the force F is the external force. Thus, using Newton’s second law (Eq. 6.1) the acceleration of the system is"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "index": 8,
          "bbox": [
            0.255,
            0.526,
            0.772,
            0.563
          ],
          "image_path": "images/page_17_equation_8.jpg",
          "content": "a = \\frac{F}{\\mathrm{mass of the system}} = \\frac{F}{\\left(m_{1} + m_{2}\\right)}\\tag{6.4}"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 9,
          "bbox": [
            0.139,
            0.568,
            0.768,
            0.587
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "The system of two boxes accelerates just like a single object of mass "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "m _ { _ 1 } + m _ { _ 2 } ."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 10,
          "bbox": [
            0.108,
            0.587,
            0.775,
            0.656
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "If you had analysed the motion of the two boxes individually as you will learn to do in higher grades, the result would have been the same. Treating connected objects as a system often simplifies the analysis. This highlights the power of Newton’s laws in studying even complicated systems of objects."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "index": 11,
          "bbox": [
            0.779,
            0.589,
            0.83,
            0.626
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "image_body",
              "index": 11,
              "bbox": [
                0.779,
                0.589,
                0.83,
                0.626
              ],
              "image_path": "images/page_17_image_11.jpg",
              "content": ""
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "index": 12,
          "bbox": [
            0.081,
            0.665,
            0.14,
            0.729
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "image_body",
              "index": 12,
              "bbox": [
                0.081,
                0.665,
                0.14,
                0.729
              ],
              "image_path": "images/page_17_image_12.jpg",
              "content": ""
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 13,
          "bbox": [
            0.155,
            0.671,
            0.364,
            0.69
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Ready to Go Beyond"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 14,
          "bbox": [
            0.138,
            0.697,
            0.633,
            0.761
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "In addition to "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "F ,"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " the external forces will be gravitational force (m "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "g + m _ { _ 2 } g )"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " acting on the system downwards, which is balanced by the normal force "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "( N _ { _ 1 } + N _ { _ 2 } )"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " acting on the system from the ground (Fig. 6.35)."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "index": 15,
          "bbox": [
            0.237,
            0.761,
            0.504,
            0.883
          ],
          "content": [
            {
